Z||000008||Mach10||08-23-2001||07:05 PM||||HEY!<BR>Fieros do <FONT SIZE="3"><B>NOT</B></FONT> leak. They mark territory. Capiche?||||reg||10
Z||000009||Mach10||08-23-2001||07:11 PM||||To find the leak, let everything dry up, pull the pump fuse, and crank the engine for a few moments. That'll depressurize the system. Let everything dry, then put the fuse back in. Look for the spreading Gas (Might have to go by smell) then go through the list of fuel-related components in that area. The Distributer was obviously the source of the flame, but maybe not anywhere near the source of the fuel.<P>I wonder: You know how Comet turns blue when wet, right? 2 things: Is this due to a reaction with water, or just the soaps dissolving? Will comet react violently with Gasoline? If it DOES turn blue with Gas, but doesn't make it explode, why not dust the suspect area to trace the leak? ||||reg||6
Z||000010||dennis_6||08-23-2001||07:14 PM||||I guess your right my fiero has not ran in a while it just needed to relive itself.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I might just take it to a shop.  I don't want to blow my car up!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by dennis_6 (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Leadfoot||08-24-2001||01:43 AM||||2 possible leak zones that happened to me are at the fuel inlet/outlet manifold (stupid me stripped the threads on the outlet port) and the cold start injector connection at the fuel rail (o-ring might be bad).  Good luck and keep a fire extingisher near by.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||dennis_6||08-25-2001||09:49 PM||||After reading the post about all the good stuff that can happen when you lose an altenator belt.  This happened in the middle of winter and my overflow tank overflowed.  I belive I will just replace the motor.<P>                      ||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||08-26-2001||08:06 PM||||To leak on that end, You've likely got an injector O ring leaking. If it's a main injector, you've got a rather large job ahead. This type repair is time consuming more than dificult.<P>easiest way I can think of to test...<P>have someone else turn on key (DO NOT start it!) while you watch/feel around the motor looking for the leak. 2-3 turns of the key should reveal the leak. (As I remember, the ECM only runs the pump so many seconds if you don't start it.)||||reg||1
